WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.072
Leadership communication should explain PQC risk without hype.

2
00:00:04.072 --> 00:00:11.032
The message is not that every encrypted record is suddenly exposed or that a single date will change everything.

3
00:00:11.032 --> 00:00:29.467
The better message is that some widely used public-key assumptions may not remain adequate in the presence of cryptographically relevant quantum computers, and that migration has enough dependency work that responsible organizations should prepare before urgency forces rushed decisions.

4
00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:32.207
Governance turns that message into action.

5
00:00:32.207 --> 00:00:45.238
Policies may need to describe approved algorithms, library expectations, certificate practices, data retention considerations, vendor requirements, exception handling, and documentation standards.

6
00:00:45.238 --> 00:00:54.863
Procurement teams may need questions for cloud providers, appliance vendors, identity platforms, managed service providers, and software suppliers.

7
00:00:54.863 --> 00:01:04.340
Risk registers should track systems with long-lived confidential data, unsupported dependencies, migration blockers, and accepted residual risk.

8
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.042
Executives usually do not need deep cryptographic theory.

9
00:01:08.042 --> 00:01:17.741
They need practical timelines, inventory progress, decisions required, funding needs, dependency risks, vendor readiness, and consequences of delay.

10
00:01:17.741 --> 00:01:33.881
A useful dashboard might show what percentage of exposed services have been inventoried, which high-value systems depend on quantum-vulnerable public-key mechanisms, which vendors have committed support, and which pilots have produced usable results.

11
00:01:33.881 --> 00:01:40.915
Training matters because many teams touch cryptography without thinking of themselves as cryptography teams.

12
00:01:40.915 --> 00:01:42.840
Developers choose libraries.

13
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.209
Platform teams manage certificates.

14
00:01:45.209 --> 00:01:48.022
Operations teams troubleshoot handshakes.

15
00:01:48.022 --> 00:01:51.428
Procurement teams ask vendors about roadmap support.

16
00:01:51.428 --> 00:01:53.205
Security teams assess risk.

17
00:01:53.205 --> 00:01:57.721
Good governance gives each group enough shared language to coordinate.

18
00:01:57.721 --> 00:02:02.904
That shared language is what prevents PQC from becoming either panic or paperwork.
